{
  "name": "Long/short position hand-off emails to the Portland Real Time shift",
  "change": "Replace the hand-typed 'we are long/short' emails with an Enpower report of each book's open positions for Real Time to fill.",
  "reason": "The proposed report already exists: Real Time's position manager already shows each book's long/short by point and hour (183.eml: 'You will see us short this amount in the RT position manager'), so what the emails add is instruction (where to fill, what to do with it, price, which deal links), which Enpower deal data does not hold; and in 120.eml Enpower itself was wrong, so an Enpower-fed report would have carried the same error.",
  "research": "Find mail showing Real Time traders re-keying or cross-checking hand-off emails against Enpower and how long that takes, and see whether the planned FSP consolidated position manager (167.eml) already covers it; a resubmission would have to target the instruction part (for example a fill-note field on the deal in Enpower) and count hand-offs from the preschedulers' mailboxes, not Bill's forwards."
}

{
  "name": "Daily email approval of the Enovate DPR",
  "change": "Automate the open-position tie-out and switch the three approvers to exception-only sign-off, with the final DPR released at a cutoff.",
  "reason": "The evidence is misread in places. 686.eml is not an approval, 221.eml shows an approver chasing the analyst rather than the analyst chasing approvals, and no reply from Tomaski or Radous is in the archive. The 10-minute daily saving is also too high: Giron's replies are mostly a one-word 'OK', and the approvers would still have to review the report.",
  "research": "Look in the Tomaski, Radous, Falcone, Abel or Luong mailboxes (if any exist) for their own replies, for the analyst chasing missing approvals, and for how much work Giron's position tie-out takes. Then re-estimate the saving using only the reply and tie-out steps, which is likely 2 to 4 minutes a day."
}

{
  "name": "Weekly check that new ERMS curves exist in RisktRAC",
  "today": "When a trader needs a new price curve, the commodity group's risk management builds it in ERMS. They then fill in a curve set-up form so that Risk Analysis and Reporting can set it up again in RisktRAC, the system that calculates VaR. Separately, they email IT to add the curve to TAGG. Desks have not always sent the form, so Risk Analysis and Reporting now checks by hand every week that all curves newly set up in ERMS are also in RisktRAC. The first check found numerous curves missing, and each has to be resolved with the desk. Until a curve is in RisktRAC, its positions are left out of VaR, so VaR is wrong.",
  "change": "A nightly report lists ERMS curves missing from RisktRAC, replacing the weekly hand check.",
  "explanation": "IT schedules a nightly job that compares the curves in ERMS with those in RisktRAC (and the TAGG mapping) and emails any ERMS curve without a match to Risk Analysis and Reporting and to the risk lead of the desk that owns it. A further step would be for a new ERMS curve to create the RisktRAC set-up request automatically, filled in from the ERMS curve definition, in place of the hand-completed form. Afterwards Risk Analysis and Reporting no longer does the weekly comparison; it reads a short exception list. Missing curves show up the next morning instead of up to a week later, so VaR is not left inaccurate in between. Only the weekly check is counted in the savings, not the form or the TAGG requests.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
      "quote": "Risk Analysis and Reporting has implemented a new process in which on a weekly basis new curves set up in ERMS are reviewed to ensure that these have been set up in RisktRAC."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
      "quote": "We did this for the first time today and noted numerous curves in ERMS that are not in RisktRAC - which we are working with you to resolve.",
      "note": "The weekly check turns up many missing curves, and each one is then followed up with the desk."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
      "quote": "If the curve is not set up in RisktRAC the positions are not captured in the VAR or engine, thereby making VAR inaccurate.",
      "note": "Why the gap matters: a curve missing from RisktRAC leaves its positions out of VaR until someone finds it."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
      "quote": "In the past few months, the procedures for setting up new curves and books have not been followed by everyone.",
      "note": "The hand-completed form is not reliably sent, which is why the weekly check was started."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
      "quote": "Risk management completes a curve set up request form to set up curve in RisktRAC",
      "note": "The same curve is set up a second time in RisktRAC from a form that is filled in by hand."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/411.eml",
      "quote": "I set up these new curves this morning and need them added to Tagg",
      "note": "New curves are also requested separately from IT for TAGG, a third system that a nightly comparison could cover."
    }
  ],
  "stalls": [],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "week",
    "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/410.eml",
    "quote": "on a weekly basis new curves set up in ERMS are reviewed"
  },
  "minutes": 35
}

{
  "name": "Monthly check of the EWS admin assistant list",
  "today": "Jessica Ramirez in the EWS Office of the Chairman keeps a spreadsheet (EEOC.xls) of the group's administrative assistants: title, location, extension, fax and supervisor. She says she will check it every month by emailing it to about 34 people. Each person has to open the file, find their own line, and reply with any corrections, and she then merges the replies by hand. Some people on the mailing are not in the pool. Bill Williams III asked to be taken off.",
  "change": "Build the monthly assistant list from the HR or address-book record, and email only the people whose entries changed or look incomplete.",
  "explanation": "Each month Ramirez pulls title, location, phone, fax and supervisor for the assistant job codes from the HR system or the Outlook address list. Those records normally carry these fields; the mail does not confirm this, so it needs checking first. She compares the pull with last month's list and emails only the few people whose entries changed or have gaps. Corrections are made in the HR or address record, so the next pull comes out right. The roughly 34 people stop getting a monthly spreadsheet to check, and people outside the pool get nothing.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/313.eml",
      "quote": "On a monthly basis I will be verifying information such as your titles, locations, extension, fax numbers, & supervisors to help Office of the Chairman for Enron Wholesale Services keep track of all the Admin Assitants in the group."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/313.eml",
      "quote": "Please let me know if the information pertaining to you is correct on the list. If it is not, please forward me the correct info.",
      "note": "Every recipient (about 34 people on the To line) has to open the spreadsheet, check their own entry and reply. Ramirez then merges the replies."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/313.eml",
      "quote": "Please remove me from this list.\nI am not involved with the management of anyone in this pool.",
      "note": "The list goes out to people who are not in the pool, so their time and hers is spent on mail that pulling the list from job codes would avoid."
    }
  ],
  "stalls": [],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "month",
    "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/313.eml",
    "quote": "On a monthly basis I will be verifying"
  },
  "minutes": 70
}
